A1. This kind of man will always shine at the top. ~ BALD
A5. The whips in the hands of the rich. ~ CROPS
A10. This is easy to get in Chicago. ~ SHOT
A14. This's wet in Canada and also in New York. ~ ERIE
A15. Turkish pipe-dream. ~ HOURI
A16. Rags, bones and hanks o' hair. ~ HAGS
A17. The last word in religion. ~ AMEN
A18. A swell name. ~ ASTOR
A19. Ground coverer. ~ AREA
A20. A New York murder case (it always remains so to the police). ~ MYSTERY
A22. An old-fashioned girl (circa 1920). ~ FLAPPER
A24. A once wealthy uncle. ~ SAM
A25. What the poet wrote and did. ~ ODE
A26. This fellow'll risk his life for an eyeful. ~ SPY
A29. Big Joe. ~ TEN
A31. Made things pretty hard to find. ~ HID
A32. This girl has exactly a half-gallon capacity. ~ GAL
A35. What little boys prefer to a bathtub. ~ POOL
A37. Spring advertiser. ~ ROBIN
A39. This didn't run fast enough ard got into an awful stew. ~ HARE
A40. Follow this and there you are! ~ ARROW
A42. Where Frogs get seasick. ~ MER
A43. A second-hand dress is one that someone's done this in. ~ SATIN
A44. Thar's gold in them thar these. ~ TEETH
A45. Stowed the stew. ~ ATE
A46. What you must do with your dough these hard times. ~ KNEAD
A47. The works in an old-time clock. ~ SAND
A49. A hot potato. ~ SPUD
A51. This gave Cleo her last kick. ~ ASP
A54. Didn't this come before the poor Indian? ~ LO
A55. A moving factor in New York's upper circles. ~ EL
A56. Kangaroo baby carriage. ~ SAC
A59. These have always proved dangerous to pedestrians. ~ STREETS
A62. Here's where many ex-bankers mess together. (Not the bread-line.) ~ ATLANTA
A64. Many a poor fish has been caught here. ~ HOOK
A65. This's quite out of place. ~ INEPT
A67. This goes over the head of the Ku-Klux Klanner. ~ HOOD
A68. Besides. ~ ELSE
A69. To zb; # m! x! ~ CURSE
A70. The puzzle eagle. ~ ERNE
A71. A tree's mama and papa. ~ SEED
A72. Laid around the henhouse. ~ EGGED
A73. Where fast males get together. ~ MEET

D1. A good home supporter. ~ BEAM
D2. Dora wants to know why if this is always standing, it doesn't sit down? ~ ARMY
D3. Golf scores. ~ LIES
D4. The real depression in the automobile business. (Pl.) ~ DENTS
D5. You girls'll find this awfully fascinating. ~ CHARMER
D6. How he found that cheeky look on school girls. ~ ROSY
D7. The safest place to be when the roof caves in. ~ OUT
D8. This guy is in the know. (Abbr.) ~ PROF
D9. A high steak. ~ SIRLOIN
D10. We're calling this an outline just as a matter of form. ~ SHAPE
D11. The instrument of the immortals (with apologies to Steinway). ~ HARP
D12. The molding you see in the puzzles. ~ OGEE
D13. When things went red he lost his head. ~ TSAR
D21. To relieve that empty feeling. ~ EAT
D23. What to do in summing up. ~ ADD
D26. Dog blanket. ~ SPAT
D27. An open and shut proposition. ~ PORE
D28. The good old days. ~ YORE
D30. A shifty fellow. ~ NOMAD
D31. Relieves the unemployment situation. ~ HIRES
D32. He kissed her on this and she gave it to him. ~ GATE
D33. What Rudy Vallee'd get if he were given the air. ~ ARIA
D34. What some men do with their lawn-mowers when spring comes. ~ LEND
D36. Sunken Florida assets. ~ LOTS
D38. To back your opinion with something more substantial than mere words. ~ BET
D39. A shaker. ~ HAND
D41. The biggest fish that ever got away. ~ WHALE
D43. Empty head. ~ SKULL
D48. An actor's best friend and severest critic. ~ NOTICE
D50. What the girl who didn't neck did. ~ PETTED
D51. What too much cigarette-smoking produces. ~ ASHES
D52. Lifted. ~ STOLE
D53. What Tolstoi's "War and Peace" is full of. ~ PROSE
D56. Make sheet music. ~ SNORE
D57. The bigger your sins the harder you'll find it to do this. ~ ATONE
D58. A movie-usher in the making. ~ CADET
D60. Stretched (housewifely speaking). ~ EKED
D61. What most love-nests are. ~ SNUG
